\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Cellular membrane organization and lipid composition underlying selective targeting. The schematic on the left illustrates a eukaryotic cell and its membrane-bound compartments, highlighting that lipid membranes are not restricted to the plasma membrane but also surround intracellular organelles, including the nucleus, endosomes, and other subcellular compartments~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {sarmento2023expanding}. These membranes possess distinct lipid compositions and may undergo compositional remodeling under pathological or altered cellular states~\blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {clinbioenerg2020009,kopecka2020phospholipids}. A magnified view of the plasma membrane is shown in the central panel, comparing representative lipid distributions in healthy mammalian, cancerous, and bacterial membranes. Healthy mammalian membranes maintain pronounced lipid asymmetry, with the outer leaflet enriched in zwitterionic phosphatidylcholine (PC; blue), whereas cancer-associated membrane remodeling may promote the externalization of anionic phosphatidylserine (PS; green). Bacterial membranes, such as those of \textit  {E.~coli}, contain substantial amounts of anionic phosphatidylglycerol (PG; red) together with phosphatidylethanolamine (PE; gray), contributing to a negatively charged membrane surface. These differences in membrane composition and surface charge provide a physicochemical basis for the preferential interaction of cationic molecules with selected membrane environments. The cellular and membrane schematic was created with BioRender \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {biorender}. The chemical structures on the right correspond to the principal lipids considered in this work: 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit  {sn}-glycero-3-phosphocholine (POPC), 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit  {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoserine (POPS), 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit  {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoglycerol (POPG), and 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit  {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine (POPE), from top to bottom. The molecular structures were rendered in ChemDraw \blx@tocontentsinit {0}\cite {chemdraw}.}}{35}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:Lipids}{{1.1}{35}{Cellular membrane organization and lipid composition underlying selective targeting. The schematic on the left illustrates a eukaryotic cell and its membrane-bound compartments, highlighting that lipid membranes are not restricted to the plasma membrane but also surround intracellular organelles, including the nucleus, endosomes, and other subcellular compartments~\cite {sarmento2023expanding}. These membranes possess distinct lipid compositions and may undergo compositional remodeling under pathological or altered cellular states~\cite {clinbioenerg2020009,kopecka2020phospholipids}. A magnified view of the plasma membrane is shown in the central panel, comparing representative lipid distributions in healthy mammalian, cancerous, and bacterial membranes. Healthy mammalian membranes maintain pronounced lipid asymmetry, with the outer leaflet enriched in zwitterionic phosphatidylcholine (PC; blue), whereas cancer-associated membrane remodeling may promote the externalization of anionic phosphatidylserine (PS; green). Bacterial membranes, such as those of \textit {E.~coli}, contain substantial amounts of anionic phosphatidylglycerol (PG; red) together with phosphatidylethanolamine (PE; gray), contributing to a negatively charged membrane surface. These differences in membrane composition and surface charge provide a physicochemical basis for the preferential interaction of cationic molecules with selected membrane environments. The cellular and membrane schematic was created with BioRender \cite {biorender}. The chemical structures on the right correspond to the principal lipids considered in this work: 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit {sn}-glycero-3-phosphocholine (POPC), 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoserine (POPS), 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoglycerol (POPG), and 1-palmitoyl-2-oleoyl-\textit {sn}-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine (POPE), from top to bottom. The molecular structures were rendered in ChemDraw \cite {chemdraw}}{figure.caption.1}{}}
